attendant, who we call <lang pt-BR>frentista</lang>, or in Bahia is…

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:46.050
<v Valdo>Yeah, it can be either <lang pt-BR>frentista</lang> or <lang pt-BR>bombeiro</lang>.

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:46.900
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Bombeiro</lang>, yeah.

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:47.960
<v Orlando>Okay, yeah.

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:49.550
<v Michelle>I've never heard about it.

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:50.390
<v Valdo>Yeah, really?

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:51.990
<v José Luis>In Venezuela, we have two words.

00:10:52.890 --> 00:11:06.300
<v Michelle>But <lang pt-BR>frentista</lang> is a person who helps us to pump the gas,
to change our oil, what else? Wash our windshields.

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:09.800
<v Valdo>So actually, all the gas stations in Brazil are full services.

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:10.980
<v Michelle>We don't have self-services.

00:11:11.030 --> 00:11:11.550
<v Valdo>Yeah.

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.880
<v Orlando>So you're just not used to getting out of your car yourself.

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:17.500
<v Michelle>No, there's always somebody there to do it for us.

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:21.050
<v Valdo>Yes, and here, it's totally different.

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:22.880
<v Orlando>Is it like that in Venezuela?

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:25.060
<v José Luis>Venezuela is full service.

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:26.250
<v Valdo>Full service too, like in Brazil.

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:28.800
<v José Luis>For security reasons.

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:33.539
<v Orlando>And I hate to say that I'm old enough to remember
the difference between full service and self service,

00:11:33.539 --> 00:11:35.907
<v Orlando>when you could drive in and choose one or the other.

00:11:35.907 --> 00:11:37.500
<v Orlando>But nowadays it's just pretty much…

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:40.000
<v Valdo>Full service.

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:46.509
<v Orlando>Okay, let's go back and let's hear the dialogue
one final time, and for everybody listening, again,

00:11:46.509 --> 00:11:52.607
<v Orlando>we want to hear those L sounds at the end of words and what
happens in the plural, words like <lang pt-BR>sinal</lang>, <lang pt-BR>sinais</lang>, <lang pt-BR>farol</lang>, <lang pt-BR>faróis</lang>,

00:11:52.607 --> 00:11:53.500
<v Orlando><lang pt-BR>difícil</lang>, <lang pt-BR>difíceis</lang>.

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:59.250
<v Orlando>And with that, Valdo, let's do it kind
of more like a native speed this time.

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:05.300
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Aquele homem está fazendo sinal</lang>
<lang pt-BR>pra gente baixar o farol do carro?</lang>

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:12.630
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Não! Vamos deixar os faróis acesos... E onde está o</lang>
<lang pt-BR>frentista pra colocar o combustível no nosso automóvel?</lang>

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:18.800
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Aqui não é tão fácil como no Brasil. Os</lang>
<lang pt-BR>automóveis são abastecidos pelo próprio motorista.</lang>

00:12:18.850 --> 00:12:22.430
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Como assim? Por que as coisas são tão difíceis aqui?</lang>

00:12:22.850 --> 00:12:26.493
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Ah, não é tão difícil assim! Pagar com o cartão e colocar a</lang>

00:12:26.493 --> 00:12:30.630
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>mangueira no carro são coisas bem</lang>
<lang pt-BR>fáceis de fazer... você consegue!</lang>

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:36.340
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>É, mas você tem que concordar que não</lang>
<lang pt-BR>há sinais claros indicando nada disso.</lang>

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:37.400
<v Orlando>There you go.

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:41.635
<v Orlando>And we confess to everybody that's listening
to <lang pt-BR>Tá Falado</lang> that when we do our lessons here,

00:12:41.635 --> 00:12:43.250
<v Orlando>we have the written word in front of us.

00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:49.490
<v Orlando>And we would suggest everybody to go do the same. Go
to our website, look at the PDF file and download it.

00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:51.500
<v Orlando>It really helps to see these words
as opposed to just listening to them,

00:12:52.850 --> 00:12:57.410
<v Orlando>and get a visual sense of these plurals that way. Okay.
I think we're at the end of our lesson today then, right?

00:12:57.670 --> 00:12:58.090
<v Michelle>Yes.

00:12:58.100 --> 00:12:58.590
<v Orlando>Good deal.

00:12:58.600 --> 00:12:59.550
<v Valdo>See you next time.

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:00.550
<v Michelle>We'll see you next time.

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:02.550
<v José Luis>Bye-bye.

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:10.750
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Tchau.</lang>
